 All links included in these posts will take you to either the library catalog record for the item in question or to additional relevant information from around the web .

 Today’s installment of Arachnophonia is by student manager Griffin (class of 2027) and features Marvin Gaye&#8217;s classic 1971 album What&#8217;s Going On . Thanks, Griffin! 

 Marvin Gaye 

 What&#8217;s Going On 

 Marvin Gaye’s critically acclaimed album, What’s Going On , has been around my music rotation apparatus for some time now. Whether it&#8217;s playing in shops, in my mom’s playlist, or playing the album on a long car drive to save my sanity, I’m familiar with Gaye’s magnum opus. That being said, when I saw the vinyl for this album newly shelved at the Parsons Music Library I made sure to sit down and give the full album a listen again (when I wasn’t 5 hours deep staring at asphalt and yellow lines). Although I shouldn’t have been surprised, it is still amazing how magical this album feels even 55 years since its release.

 Marvin Gaye’s beautifully emotional voice and tonal range make it impossible to not be a pleasure for the ear. His soothingly melodic vocal performance contrasts the very serious topics covered in the lyrics. Discussing the Vietnam war, race, poverty, peace, religion, environment, the whole album makes social issues the focus. Finally, maybe the most iconic piece of this album is its extraordinary orchestral production. It truly gives the feeling of a huge production and captures magic in a bottle. It’s satisfying picking out all of the instruments being used. (For any fans of the Silk Sonic album, the production is reminiscent of this album.) I highly recommend everyone give What&#8217;s Going On a listen, and check out this vinyl at the Parsons Music Library !

